# AWS CloudFormation – S3 Bucket

## 📌 Project Overview

This project demonstrates **Infrastructure as Code (IaC)** using **AWS CloudFormation**. An S3 bucket was created and managed using a CloudFormation YAML template instead of creating the resource manually through the AWS Console.

## 🛠️ AWS Services Used

* **AWS CloudFormation**
* **Amazon S3**

## 🎯 Objectives

* Understand Infrastructure as Code (IaC)
* Learn the basics of AWS CloudFormation Templates (CFT)
* Create an S3 bucket using a YAML template
* Create and manage a CloudFormation stack
* Perform CloudFormation drift detection

## 📂 Project Structure

```text
AWS-CloudFormation-S3/
└── template.yaml
```

## ⚙️ Implementation

### 1. Created a CloudFormation Template

A YAML template was created to define an S3 bucket as an AWS resource.

### 2. Created a CloudFormation Stack

The YAML template was uploaded to AWS CloudFormation and used to create a stack.

The stack automatically created the S3 bucket defined in the template.

### 3. Verified the S3 Bucket

The created S3 bucket was verified through the Amazon S3 console.

### 4. Performed Drift Detection

CloudFormation drift detection was used to check whether the deployed S3 resource remained consistent with the configuration defined in the CloudFormation template.

## 🔄 Workflow

```text
CloudFormation YAML Template
          ↓
   CloudFormation Stack
          ↓
       S3 Bucket
          ↓
    Drift Detection
          ↓
   Resource Validation
```

## 📚 Key Learnings

* CloudFormation allows AWS infrastructure to be defined as code.
* YAML can be used to describe AWS resources.
* A CloudFormation stack manages the resources defined in a template.
* Infrastructure can be created consistently without manually configuring every resource.
* Drift detection helps identify differences between the expected CloudFormation configuration and the actual resource configuration.

## 🚀 Future Improvements

* Add more AWS resources such as EC2, VPC, and IAM.
* Create a complete multi-resource infrastructure using CloudFormation.
* Compare AWS CloudFormation with Terraform.
* Add parameters and outputs to make the template reusable.

